Use $a$ for the acetyl methyl, $b$ for the O-bound <methylene group> pair, $c$ for the internal vinyl <proton>, and $d,e$ for the terminal vinyl <protons> respectively trans and cis to $c$. The assignments are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c|l}
\text{protons}&\delta_H/\mathrm{ppm}&\text{integral}&\text{resolved pattern}\\\hline
a&2.081&3&\text{singlet}\\
b&4.570&2&\text{doublet of triplets}\\
c&5.919&1&\text{doublet of doublets of triplets}\\
d&5.314&1&\text{doublet of apparent quartets}\\
e&5.235&1&\text{doublet of apparent quartets}
\end{array}
$$
The two terminal <protons> are <diastereotopic>: one is cis and the other trans to $c$, so they have different shifts and different <vicinal couplings>. Each terminal <proton> also couples through <geminal coupling> to the other and through <long-range scalar coupling> to the two $b$ <protons>. When those small couplings coincide, an underlying <doublet of doublets> of <NMR triplets> collapses to the observed <NMR doublet> of apparent <NMR quartets>. The O-bound <protons> are <enantiotopic> and have <chemical equivalence in NMR> in this achiral environment; they are split by $c$ and by the two terminal <protons>. The integrated terminal envelope contains two <protons>, not two equivalent <protons> at one shift.

\Image[paper-1-allyl-couplings.png]
{title=Original stereochemically labelled proton structure for C with all resolved vicinal, geminal and long-range couplings}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The large vinyl couplings are 17.1 Hz trans and 10.5 Hz cis; the O-methylene-to-vinyl coupling is 6.0 Hz.** At this field, convert every separation by $J/\mathrm{Hz}=300\,\Delta\delta/\mathrm{ppm}$. Comparing the centres of corresponding terminal-proton submultiplets gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
{}^3J_{cd}&=300(5.350-5.293)=\boxed{17.1\,\mathrm{Hz}},\\
{}^3J_{ce}&=300(5.260-5.225)=\boxed{10.5\,\mathrm{Hz}},\\
{}^3J_{bc}&=300(4.585-4.565)=\boxed{6.0\,\mathrm{Hz}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The repeated 0.005-ppm fine spacing from <allylic scalar coupling> and <geminal coupling> gives the magnitudes
$$
\boxed{|{}^2J_{de}|\simeq|{}^4J_{bd}|\simeq|{}^4J_{be}|\simeq1.5\,\mathrm{Hz}.}
$$
For example, splitting by a geminal <proton> and two equivalent long-range-coupled methylene <protons>, all with the same magnitude, produces overlapping lines with intensities $1:3:3:1$. The 6-Hz splitting of $b$ creates two such small <NMR triplets> centred at 4.580 and 4.560 ppm. An independent check comes from the internal-vinyl multiplet: a centre at 5.919 ppm with splittings 17.1, 10.5 and 6.0 Hz, the last to two equivalent <protons>, reproduces all twelve labelled line positions. The spectrum determines these coupling magnitudes, not their algebraic signs.

