= Solution
{parent=i}

**The products lie below the reactants, while the <transition state> lies above both.** On an energy-versus-reaction-coordinate plot, the forward <activation energy> is the vertical separation $E_{\rm TS}-E_{\rm R}$, not the difference between product and reactant energies. For an exothermic elementary path, $\Delta E=E_{\rm P}-E_{\rm R}<0$, and the reverse barrier is $E_{a,-}=E_{a,+}-\Delta E>E_{a,+}$.

\Image[paper-1-exothermic-profile.png]
{title=Original schematic exothermic energy profile with reactant, product, transition-state and forward-barrier labels}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Arrhenius equation> is
$$
\boxed{k(T)=A\exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{RT}\right)}.
$$
The exponential gives the strongly temperature-dependent energetic factor. For a simple bimolecular collision picture, $A$ collects the encounter frequency per reactant <concentration> and the fraction of encounters with a suitable orientation and other dynamical requirements. Thus a collision is not automatically a successful reaction even when its energy is sufficient.

$A$ has the same units as $k$, which depend on the <rate law>; it is not always a frequency in $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. More generally it is an effective prefactor containing statistical and dynamical contributions rather than a literal universal collision count. Treating it as <temperature> independent is the approximation requested here.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Rearrange the Arrhenius relation using the molar <activation energy> and the <molar gas constant>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
A&=k_3\exp\left(\frac{E_a}{RT}\right)\\
&=(1.933\times10^{-14})\exp\left(\frac{4905}{8.3145\times298}\right),\\
\boxed{A}&=\boxed{1.400\times10^{-13}\,\mathrm{dm^3\,molecule^{-1}\,s^{-1}}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The exponent is dimensionless. There is no Avogadro factor in it because both $E_a$ and $R$ are expressed per mole; the molecular <concentration> convention only determines the units of the prefactor.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For forward and reverse elementary reactions at equilibrium, the two rates are equal. Both directions have two reactant particles and use the same <concentration> convention, so the conversion factors cancel and $K_{\rm eq}=k_3/k_{-3}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{k_{-3}=\frac{1.933\times10^{-14}}{7.244\times10^{33}}
=2.668\times10^{-48}\,\mathrm{dm^3\,molecule^{-1}\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
For molar rather than molecular number <concentration>, the bimolecular <rate constant> is multiplied by the <Avogadro constant>:
$$
\boxed{k_{-3}=2.668\times10^{-48}(6.022\times10^{23})
=1.607\times10^{-24}\,\mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
Only one factor is needed: converting the two <concentration> factors contributes $N_A^2$, while converting the production rate from <molecules> to moles removes one factor.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**Step 1 is initiation; steps 2 and 3 are propagation; step 4 is termination.** Initiation dissociates a <chlorine> <molecule> into two <chlorine> <free radicals>. Each propagation step consumes one <free radical> and creates another, carrying the chain forward without destroying its <free radical> carrier overall. Termination removes two <chlorine> <free radicals> by recombination.

The inert collision partner accepts or supplies energy as needed and is regenerated. It is not a <free radical> chain carrier or a net reagent. Adding the two propagation steps cancels the <hydrogen> and <chlorine> <free radicals> and gives one complete conversion of <hydrogen> plus <chlorine> to two <molecules> of <hydrogen chloride>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The reverse elementary <rate constant> is extraordinarily small at 298 K.** Its reaction would require <hydrogen chloride> and a <chlorine> <free radical> to produce a <hydrogen> <free radical> and <chlorine>, reversing a highly exothermic propagation step. The value found above makes this channel negligible for the intended reacting mixture compared with forward propagation.

A large <equilibrium constant> alone does not permit a reverse reaction to be ignored under every conceivable composition: if forward reactants were exhausted, <concentration> ratios would matter. The simplification applies during the forward chain-reaction regime specified, rather than constituting a universal statement about all late-time or imposed reverse conditions.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**Apply the <steady-state approximation in chemical kinetics> to the <hydrogen> and <chlorine> <free radicals>.** After a short initiation transient their formation and consumption can balance while the stable reactants continue to be depleted and <hydrogen chloride> accumulates. Accordingly set $\mathrm d[\mathrm H]/\mathrm dt\simeq0$ and $\mathrm d[\mathrm{Cl}]/\mathrm dt\simeq0$.

This does not mean the <free radicals> are absent or that the whole mixture is at equilibrium. <hydrogen> and <chlorine> <molecules> and the <hydrogen chloride> product generally cannot all be assigned steady concentrations over appreciable conversion. The inert-body <concentration> can be treated as an imposed or slowly varying bath quantity; it is not an intermediate to which a <free radical> balance must be applied.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Let $r_i$ be the event rate for step $i$, so
$$
r_1=k_1[\mathrm M][\mathrm{Cl_2}],\quad
r_2=k_2[\mathrm{Cl}][\mathrm{H_2}],\quad
r_3=k_3[\mathrm H][\mathrm{Cl_2}],\quad
r_4=k_4[\mathrm{Cl}]^2[\mathrm M].
$$
The <free radical> balances, including the factors of two for initiation and termination, are
$$
\begin{aligned}
0&\simeq r_2-r_3,\\
0&\simeq2r_1-r_2+r_3-2r_4.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus $r_2=r_3$ and $r_1=r_4$, giving
$$
[\mathrm{Cl}]=\sqrt{\frac{k_1[\mathrm{Cl_2}]}{k_4}},\qquad
[\mathrm H]=\frac{k_2[\mathrm{Cl}][\mathrm{H_2}]}{k_3[\mathrm{Cl_2}]}.
$$
Both propagation events form <hydrogen chloride>, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mathrm d[\mathrm{HCl}]}{\mathrm dt}
=2k_2\sqrt{\frac{k_1}{k_4}}[\mathrm{H_2}][\mathrm{Cl_2}]^{1/2}.}
$$
**The orders are one in <hydrogen>, one half in <chlorine> and three halves overall**, with $k_{\rm obs}=2k_2\sqrt{k_1/k_4}$ when the observed rate is HCl formation. If the rate is instead defined as the stoichiometrically normalized reaction rate, half that formation rate, the prefactor lacks the factor two. The bath <concentration> cancels because the same M-assisted initiation and recombination control the <free radical> pool. The result does not require $k_2=k_3$; different <free radical> concentrations make their steady fluxes equal. For molar <concentration>, $k_{\rm obs}$ has units $\mathrm{dm^{3/2}\,mol^{-1/2}\,s^{-1}}$.

= v
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Using bonds broken minus bonds formed, the enthalpies per mole of elementary events are
$$
\begin{array}{c|r}
\text{step}&\Delta H_i/\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\\\hline
1&+242\\
2&436-431=+5\\
3&242-431=-189\\
4&-242
\end{array}
$$
Under the <steady-state approximation in chemical kinetics>, initiation and termination have equal event rates, so their <enthalpy> contributions cancel. The two propagation steps also have equal flux, and their combined <enthalpy> is $5-189=\boxed{-184\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}}$ per overall reaction producing two moles of HCl. **Step 3 dominates the <heat> release**; the small endothermic contribution of step 2 only slightly offsets it.

An insulated vessel cannot remove this released energy as <heat>, so its <temperature> rises as reaction proceeds. In a rigid vessel the precise energy balance uses reaction <internal energy> and constant-volume <heat> capacities, whereas a constant-pressure adiabatic balance uses <enthalpy>; the strong exothermic conclusion is the same. Warming changes the <rate constants>, and activated initiation and propagation can accelerate, producing positive thermal feedback and potentially a rapid <temperature> rise. A numerical final <temperature> or a claim of an inevitable explosion is not justified without composition, <heat> capacities and heat-loss information. The initially assumed 298-K <rate constants> cannot remain fixed throughout an appreciable adiabatic <temperature> rise.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**The large drop from <carbon> to <silicon> is the main trend; the smaller reversals at Ge and Pb reflect changes in shielding and valence-electron binding.** <Allen electronegativity> is based on the mean <binding energy> of <valence electrons> in free ground-state atoms, rather than an arbitrary charge-transfer count. Moving to larger principal shells generally makes the <valence electrons> more diffuse and more shielded, reducing their attraction to the nucleus. <carbon>'s compact second-shell valence orbitals therefore give particularly high <electronegativity>.

The intervening filled 3d shell shields imperfectly, increasing effective nuclear attraction in <germanium> relative to a simple smooth extrapolation from <silicon>. This helps explain Ge being slightly above Si rather than below it. Increased size then lowers Sn relative to Ge. The filled inner d and f shells and relativistic stabilization of <lead>'s 6s <electrons> modify the next step; contraction and stronger binding of the 6s contribution help Pb lie slightly above Sn. The small reversals should not obscure the much larger C-to-Si change. The spectroscopic definition includes both occupied s and p valence contributions, so the relativistic s effect can influence the Allen average even though not every electron participates equally in every bond.<Electronegativity is the average one-electron energy of the valence-shell electrons in ground-state free atoms by Leland C. Allen>[Definition of the valence-shell spectroscopic average]{ref}

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**The valence configurations are $\mathrm C:2s^22p^2$, $\mathrm{Si}:3s^23p^2$, and $\mathrm{Pb}:6s^26p^2$.** Their full closed-shell cores are respectively $[\mathrm{He}]$, $[\mathrm{Ne}]$, and $[\mathrm{Xe}]4f^{14}5d^{10}$. The filled 4f and 5d subshells of <lead> are not the outer valence configuration requested.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**<carbon> uses strong 2s/2p-derived covalent bonding, whereas retention or involvement of the low-energy Pb 6s pair distinguishes the two <lead> oxides.** In CO, <carbon> has an approximately triple-bonded description with one <carbon> <lone pair>; the latter has substantial 2s character, while the sigma and two pi bonding combinations involve the valence shell, particularly <carbon> 2p orbitals for the pi bonds. In CO$_2$, <carbon> is formally in <oxidation state> +4 and its four <valence electrons> participate in two strongly covalent C=O double-bond frameworks. A localized account uses sp hybrids for sigma bonding and two <p orbitals> for the pi systems. <oxidation> states are bookkeeping devices here: neither <molecule> contains a bare C$^{2+}$ or C$^{4+}$ <ion>.

In PbO, the Pb(II) description removes or employs mainly the two 6p <electrons>, retaining a relatively low-energy 6s$^2$ pair. In PbO$_2$, the Pb(IV) description additionally involves those two 6s <electrons>. The stabilization and poor availability of that pair underlie the <inert-pair effect> and the relative preference for Pb(II) chemistry. “Inert” does not mean that the 6s <electrons> have zero influence on bonding or shape: mixing with <oxygen> states and empty Pb states can make the Pb(II) <lone pair> stereochemically active. The more ionic formal electron counts in the <lead> oxides and the covalent molecular-orbital description of the <carbon> oxides are complementary models, not literal assignments of individual <electrons> to immutable <atomic orbitals>.<The origin of the stereochemically active Pb(II) lone pair: DFT calculations on PbO and PbS by Aron Walsh and Graeme W. Watson>[Abstract and electronic-density analysis: oxygen-dependent lone-pair activity in PbO]{ref}

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**CO and CO$_2$ are discrete <molecules>, whereas the other oxides gain stability from extended bonding or ionic lattices.** <carbon>'s compact 2p orbitals overlap effectively with <oxygen> 2p orbitals, allowing strong multiple bonds that satisfy the valence requirements within small <molecules>. Intermolecular forces between CO or CO$_2$ <molecules> are much weaker than those internal bonds, so low condensation temperatures and gaseous states at room conditions are unsurprising. CO$_2$ is nonpolar overall but still has <London dispersion forces> and interactions between <electric quadrupoles>; “nonpolar” does not mean no attraction.

For larger Group 14 atoms, overlap with <oxygen> 2p orbitals is less effective for strong localized multiple bonding. SiO$_2$ instead forms an extended network of SiO$_4$ <tetrahedra> with each <oxygen> bridging two <silicon> atoms. Breaking or melting the network involves much more than separating intact SiO$_2$ <molecules>. The Si–O <electronegativity> difference is $3.61-1.92=1.69$, so these are strongly polar <covalent bonds> rather than evidence for a molecular solid.

For Pb–O the difference is $3.61-1.85=1.76$, and ionic Pb$^{2+}$/O$^{2-}$ or Pb$^{4+}$/O$^{2-}$ descriptions provide useful starting points, with appreciable covalent contributions. PbO has layered structures with asymmetric <oxygen> coordination consistent with a stereochemically active 6s$^2$ pair. A common PbO$_2$ structure is rutile-like, with PbO$_6$ octahedra linked through <oxygen>. Extended lattice interactions account for their solid states.https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.107.246402[Geometry and Electronic Structure: rutile PbO2, six-coordinate Pb and three-coordinate O]{ref} The similar Si–O and Pb–O <electronegativity> differences show why a single difference threshold cannot determine the entire bonding type, <coordination number> or structure.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**A suitable representative structure is a three-dimensional network of corner-sharing CO$_4$ <tetrahedra>, with C–O single bonds.** Each <carbon> is four-coordinate; every bridging <oxygen> is bonded to two carbons. Sharing four oxygens between two <tetrahedra> gives $4/2=2$ oxygens per <carbon>, so the network retains empirical formula CO$_2$ without containing separate O=C=O <molecules>.

High <pressure> favours a compact, highly coordinated network through its smaller molar volume: at fixed <temperature>, increasing <pressure> changes relative Gibbs energies according to $\mathrm d(\Delta G)=\Delta V\,\mathrm dp$. Heating helps overcome the large reconstructive barrier to breaking and redistributing molecular pi bonding and establishing an extended single-bond network. It should not be argued that high <temperature> by itself favours the lower-entropy network. Nor is merely freezing molecular CO$_2$ sufficient: dry ice remains a molecular solid.

The sketch shows connectivity and local tetrahedral coordination, not a unique predicted <crystal structure>. Real <amorphous carbonia> need not have perfect periodic order or exclusively one coordination environment. The original <carbonia> experiments combined high-pressure preparation with infrared, Raman and diffraction evidence for loss of molecular character and formation of an amorphous network.<Amorphous silica-like carbon dioxide by Mario Santoro and others>[Abstract: spectroscopy and diffraction evidence for amorphous nonmolecular CO2]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-carbonia-network.png]
{title=Original representative corner-sharing tetrahedral CO2 network fragment; terminal bonds continue into the solid}

= e
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

**Amine <nitrogen> binds the <carbon> end of CO$_2$; Mg$^{2+}$ binds through <oxygen>.** The polarized C=O bonds make <carbon> electrophilic and <oxygen> electron rich. An amine <nitrogen> <lone pair> therefore donates toward <carbon>, potentially giving a carbamate-like adduct after <proton transfer>. The hard <Lewis acid> Mg$^{2+}$ accepts an <oxygen> <lone pair>, either from coordinated CO$_2$ or from an <oxygen> of a captured <carbamate> species. Simply reversing these assignments because <nitrogen> and <oxygen> both have <lone pairs> would put two electron-pair donors together without accounting for the electrophilic site.

The local binding pattern does not by itself determine the full proton-transfer state or whether cooperative chains form; those depend on the framework and amine arrangement. A documented diamine-appended framework shows cooperative carbon-dioxide insertion and <ammonium carbamate chain> formation, illustrating one possible implementation.<Cooperative insertion of CO2 in diamine-appended metal-organic frameworks by Thomas M. McDonald and others>[Adsorption mechanism and ammonium-carbamate-chain assignment]{ref}

To regenerate the material, lower the CO$_2$ <chemical potential> using reduced <pressure> or a CO$_2$-free purge, and, where binding is exothermic, <heat> the framework to favour <desorption>. The <temperature> must remain compatible with framework stability. This reverses <adsorption> or reversible <chemisorption>; it does not require permanently destroying the <metal–organic framework>.
